<!doctype html>
{% import "_brand.html.j2" as brand %}
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>About · Qaiet Times</title>
<meta name="description" content="How Qaiet Times works: where the news comes from, how stories are grouped, ranked and labeled, and what the labels mean.">
{{ brand.favicon() }}
{{ brand.theme_boot() }}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ root }}static/style.css">
</head>
<body>
<div class="page about">

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="masthead-top">
    {{ brand.wordmark(root) }}
    {{ brand.nav(root, "about") }}
  </div>
  <hr class="rule">
</header>

<main class="prose doc">
<h1>How this site works</h1>
<p class="lede">Every morning around {{ a.publish_at }} Stockholm time, this page answers three questions: what happened in AI, how the press is framing it, and what people who build AI are actually making. It tries to do that in about five minutes of reading, without ads or noise.</p>

{% if a.desks|length > 1 %}
<section id="desks">
  <h2>Desks</h2>
  <p>The site has {{ a.desks|length }} desks: {% for d in a.desks %}<a href="{{ root }}{{ d.prefix }}">{{ d.name }}</a>{% if not loop.last %}{{ ", " if loop.revindex > 2 else " and " }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}. Each is a full page of its own, with its stories, sentiment, narratives, index and archive. Every news item is sorted onto one desk, or left out: AI first by keywords, everything else by a language model reading the headline. Outlets that cover only one subject (a solar trade magazine, a space news site) go straight to that desk. Builders (GitHub, Hugging Face, Hacker News, Bluesky) are followed for AI only, so Rising and The gap are on the AI desk.</p>
  <p>Stance always means stance toward the desk's own subject, so the desks' figures are never added up or compared.</p>
  <ul class="facts">
    {% for d in a.desks if d.subject %}<li><strong>{{ d.name }}:</strong> {{ d.subject }}. Stance: {{ d.stance }}.</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p>The other desks are new. Their labels are not yet checked against hand labels the way AI's are.</p>
  {% if a.energy_data %}
  <p>The energy desk also shows today's day-ahead power price for {% for x in a.energy_data.areas %}{{ x }}{% if not loop.last %}{{ ", " if loop.revindex > 2 else " and " }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}: the day's mean, lowest and highest quarter-hour price in euros per megawatt-hour, and the daily mean over the last {{ a.energy_data.days }} days. The prices are set by the power exchanges' day-ahead auction and published by Energinet, Denmark's grid operator, on <a href="https://www.energidataservice.dk/">Energi Data Service</a> under CC BY 4.0 (Source: Energinet). They are fetched once each morning, with no model involved, and are figures beside the news: never labeled and never part of the sentiment index.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}

<nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
  {% if a.desks|length > 1 %}<a href="#desks">Desks</a>{% endif %}<a href="#sources">Sources</a><a href="#steps">How a page is made</a><a href="#labels">Reading the labels</a><a href="#checking">Checking the labels</a><a href="#ranking">Ranking</a><a href="#gap">The gap</a><a href="#index">Sentiment index</a><a href="#narratives">Narratives</a><a href="#periods">Time periods</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a><a href="#privacy">Privacy and cost</a><a href="#health">Source health</a>
</nav>

<section id="sources">
  <h2>Where the news comes from</h2>
  <p>Everything is collected from free, public sources. Nothing is behind a login or paid for.</p>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.outlets }} news outlets</strong> in {{ a.counts.countries }} countries and {{ a.counts.languages }} languages, read through their own RSS feeds.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.gdelt }} more outlets</strong> with no usable feed ({{ a.gdelt_outlets|map(attribute="name")|join(", ") }}), read through the GDELT news index. For these only the headline and date are available.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.aggregators }} editor-picked aggregators</strong> ({{ a.aggregators|join(" and ") }}), used only to tell which stories editors are leading with.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.digests }} AI newsletters that summarize the day</strong> and <strong>{{ a.counts.commentary }} commentary newsletters</strong> from researchers, critics, policy writers and investors.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.labs }} official AI lab blogs</strong>, attached to stories as the primary source.</li>
    <li><strong>Builder sources:</strong> new and fast-growing GitHub repositories, Hugging Face's trending models, datasets, spaces, daily papers and posts, Hacker News, new models on OpenRouter, Product Hunt's AI launches, and {{ a.counts.bluesky }} curated Bluesky accounts. Posts on X and Reddit threads appear only second-hand, credited to AINews: when its daily recap links them next to a story's article or a project's page, or when one of its bullets about X posts matches a story's topic. Those X posts also count toward the social signal in the ranking.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We only keep each item's headline, a short summary (at most 500 characters), its link and its date. We never copy articles, never go around paywalls, and show no images. Every headline links to the original publisher. Sites whose robots.txt asks automated readers to stay away are left out, not worked around.{% if a.opt_out %} {{ a.opt_out|join(", ") }} {{ "asks" if a.opt_out|length == 1 else "ask" }} that its text not be used as input to AI systems, so its headlines are shown and counted but never labeled.{% endif %}</p>

  <details class="source-list">
    <summary>All {{ a.counts.outlets }} news outlets</summary>
    {% for g in a.groups %}
    <h3>{{ g.label }}</h3>
    <p>{% for o in g.outlets %}{{ o.name }} <span class="country">{{ o.country }}{% if o.language != "en" %}, {{ o.language }}{% endif %}{% if o.type == "state-aligned" %}, state-aligned{% endif %}</span>{% if not loop.last %}<span class="sep">·</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
    {% endfor %}
    <h3>Through GDELT</h3>
    <p>{% for o in a.gdelt_outlets %}{{ o.name }} <span class="country">{{ o.country }}{% if o.type == "state-aligned" %}, state-aligned{% endif %}</span>{% if not loop.last %}<span class="sep">·</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
    <h3>Newsletters</h3>
    <p>{{ a.newsletters|join(" · ") }}</p>
    <h3>Lab blogs</h3>
    <p>{{ a.labs|join(" · ") }}</p>
  </details>
  {% if a.ainews_x %}
  <details class="source-list">
    <summary>X accounts in AINews ({{ a.ainews_x.accounts|length }})</summary>
    <p>We don't read X. AINews, one of the digests above, summarizes AI posts on X. These are the accounts whose posts it linked in its last {{ a.ainews_x.issues }} issues (counted on {{ a.ainews_x.as_of }}), most linked first, with the number of posts.</p>
    <p>{% for x in a.ainews_x.accounts %}<span style="white-space: nowrap"><a href="https://x.com/{{ x.handle }}" rel="noopener">@{{ x.handle }}</a> <span class="country">{{ x.posts }}</span></span>{% if not loop.last %} <span class="sep">·</span> {% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
  </details>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="steps">
  <h2>How a page is made</h2>
  <p>Sources are collected every {{ a.collect_every_hours }} hours through the day and night, so nothing scrolls off a busy feed before we see it. Every morning from {{ a.publish_at }} (GitHub sometimes starts scheduled runs late, so it can be a little later) the rest of the chain runs:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Collect.</strong> Every source is fetched politely: one request at a time per site, a clear name in every request, and no refetching of feeds that haven't changed.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep what is about AI.</strong> Items from AI-only sources are kept as they are. Others are checked for AI terms in {{ a.keyword_languages }} languages, and the unclear cases are decided by a small language model ({{ a.item_model }}). Headlines in other languages are translated into English for display; the original stays one hover away.</li>
    <li><strong>Group into stories.</strong> Each headline is turned into a numeric fingerprint of its meaning with a multilingual model that runs alongside the pipeline, not through an outside service ({{ a.embed_model.split("/")[-1] }}), so a Swedish and an English report of the same event land next to each other. Links count first: a Hacker News post or a Bluesky post that links to an article joins that article's story. Then headlines join the story they are closest to, but only when they are very close or share a name such as a company or a person. Stories stay open for {{ a.window_hours }} hours after their latest item.</li>
    <li><strong>Label each article.</strong> The language model reads only the headline and the start of the summary, never the outlet's name, and labels the angle, the framing and the stance (see below). It must quote the words that show its reasoning; a quote that is not really in the text is thrown out.</li>
    <li><strong>Rank.</strong> Stories are scored by how widely they are being picked up (see Ranking).</li>
    <li><strong>Summarize.</strong> For the top stories, a larger model ({{ a.story_model }}) writes a neutral one-line description of what happened, a sentence on how the coverage splits, and a sentence on what builders are doing. It also writes the one-line summary of the day at the top.</li>
    <li><strong>Build the page.</strong> The result is a plain static page, rebuilt from scratch every morning. Yesterday's page moves to the archive.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="labels">
  <h2>Reading the labels</h2>
  <p>Each article gets three labels. They describe how the article talks about its subject, not whether it is right.</p>

  <h3>Angle</h3>
  <dl class="defs">
    <dt>Technical</dt><dd>Models, research, capabilities, benchmarks, tools, safety research. Framed as <em>hype</em> (bigger or nearer than shown), <em>grounded</em> (claims matched to evidence, or plain reporting) or <em>skeptical</em> (questioning the claims or the value).</dd>
    <dt>Political</dt><dd>Regulation, policy, geopolitics, chips and export controls, elections, courts. Framed as <em>pro-regulation</em>, <em>pro-acceleration</em>, <em>national competition</em> (a race between countries) or <em>neutral</em>.</dd>
    <dt>Economic</dt><dd>Funding, valuations, data centers, earnings, jobs, adoption. Framed as <em>boom</em>, <em>bubble</em>, <em>disruption concern</em> (harm to jobs or industries) or <em>neutral</em>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>Stance</h3>
  <p>The article's attitude toward AI or the AI industry in that story. Plain factual reporting is neutral even when the news is good or bad for someone.</p>
  <ul class="stance-key">
    <li><span class="dot st-2"></span><strong>Strongly critical</strong>: alarmed or hostile.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st-1"></span><strong>Critical</strong>: worried or skeptical.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st0"></span><strong>Neutral</strong>: descriptive or balanced.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st1"></span><strong>Positive</strong>: optimistic.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st2"></span><strong>Enthusiastic</strong>: strongly positive or promotional.</li>
    <li><span class="dot stunclear"></span><strong>Unclear</strong>: the model wasn't confident, or couldn't point to words in the text. We would rather say so than guess.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The colors are deliberately not red and blue: stance here has nothing to do with left and right. The small bar on each story shows the mix across all of its coverage, the browser icon on each card opens its top source (the official post when a lab published one, otherwise the article closest to the story's center, an English original first), and "Expand Sources" lists one headline per outlet, grouped by stance, with the quoted words the label rests on, so you can judge for yourself.</p>
</section>

<section id="checking">
  <h2>Checking the labels</h2>
  {% set ag = a.agreement %}
  {% if ag.n %}
  <p>A person labeled {{ ag.n }} articles by hand, chosen at random across angles and stances, without seeing the model's labels. The model agrees with them on the angle {{ ag.track.agree }}% of the time{% if ag.frame.n %}, on the frame {{ ag.frame.agree }}% (where both chose the same angle){% endif %}, and on the stance {{ ag.stance_exact.agree }}% exactly and {{ ag.stance_within_1.agree }}% within one step. The hand labels are in the repository, so you can check them too.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p>A blind sample of articles, spread across angles and stances, is being labeled by hand to measure how often the model agrees with a person. The numbers will appear here once that is done.</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% set cx = a.crosscheck %}
  {% if cx.n %}
  <p>A second opinion from a larger model: {{ cx.model }} labeled the same {{ cx.n }} articles, from the same instructions and the same headline and summary. It agrees with the labels on this site on the angle {{ cx.track.agree }}% of the time{% if cx.frame.n %}, on the frame {{ cx.frame.agree }}% (where both chose the same angle){% endif %}, and on the stance {{ cx.stance_exact.agree }}% exactly and {{ cx.stance_within_1.agree }}% within one step.{% if cx.stance_bias is not none %} {% if cx.stance_bias|abs < 0.1 %}Neither reads the coverage as more critical than the other.{% else %}On average the site's labels read {{ "%.1f"|format(cx.stance_bias|abs) }} of a step more {{ "positive" if cx.stance_bias > 0 else "critical" }} than {{ cx.model }}'s.{% endif %}{% endif %} This catches plain mistakes and shows how much a single label can wobble. It can't catch a lean that both models share, since they come from the same family; that is what the hand labels are for.</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if a.audit %}
  <p>Another check looks for skew: does the model call one group of outlets critical far more than the others? Average stance of confident labels, by outlet group, across {{ a.audit_labels|thousands }} labeled articles so far:</p>
  <table class="weights audit">
    <thead><tr><th>Outlets</th><th class="num">Articles</th><th class="num">Average</th><th class="num">Critical</th><th class="num">Positive</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for r in a.audit %}<tr><td>{{ r.label }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.n|thousands }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.mean|signed }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.critical }}%</td><td class="num">{{ r.positive }}%</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Differences between groups are expected: opinion sections and public broadcasters do write about AI differently from the trade press. What would point to a problem is a group whose hand labels and model labels disagree in one direction. The hand-labeled sample is what tells those apart.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="ranking">
  <h2>Ranking</h2>
  <p>A story ranks high when many different kinds of sources pick it up, not when one source shouts. Each signal is scaled from 0 to 1 across the stories in view, then weighted:</p>
  <table class="weights">
    <tbody>
    {% for w in a.weights %}<tr><td>{{ w.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ w.weight }}%</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Counts like outlets and countries grow on a gentle curve, so 14 outlets stays well ahead of 2. Signals from a single platform, like Hacker News points or GitHub stars, are ranked rather than added up, so a viral thread can't outweigh broad coverage. Older activity counts for less, more gently in longer views.</p>
  <p><strong>Most mentioned</strong> lists the names (companies, people, products, places) that the most outlets mentioned in the period, each outlet counted once. <strong>Rising</strong> lists the builder projects gaining the most stars, likes or upvotes, grouped by what they are.</p>
</section>

<section id="gap">
  <h2>The gap</h2>
  <p>Two short lists on the front page show where builders and the press are looking at different things.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Builders are talking, press isn't:</strong> projects and threads in the top {{ 100 - a.gap.builder_min_pct }}% of the period on Hacker News points, GitHub stars per day or Hugging Face trending, with real volume behind them (at least {{ a.gap.floors.hn_points }} points, {{ a.gap.floors.star_velocity }} stars a day or a trending score of {{ a.gap.floors.hf_trending }}), that {{ "no outlet" if a.gap.quiet_max_outlets == 0 else "at most one outlet" }} covered.</li>
    <li><strong>Press is talking, builders aren't:</strong> stories covered by {{ a.gap.loud_min_outlets }} or more outlets with little or no discussion on Hacker News, GitHub, Hugging Face or Bluesky.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Neither list is a judgment. A model release the press missed may simply be early; a policy story builders ignore may matter a great deal.</p>
</section>

<section id="index">
  <h2>Sentiment index</h2>
  <p>One number per day for how positive or critical the coverage of AI is, from −2 to +2, overall and for each angle. Each outlet's labels for the day are averaged first (confident labels count fully, medium-confidence ones {{ (a.sentiment.medium_weight * 100)|round|int }}%, unclear ones not at all), then the outlets are averaged, so prolific outlets don't dominate. A day needs at least {{ a.sentiment.min_outlets }} outlets to get a value. The front page shows the average for the period you're viewing; the <a href="{{ root }}sentiment/">index page</a> has the daily history and a table. When you filter the front page by a region or a country, the figures change to the index from the outlets there only, worked out the same way, so a day counts only when at least {{ a.sentiment.min_outlets }} of them were labeled.</p>
</section>

<section id="narratives">
  <h2>Narratives</h2>
  <p>A story the press has covered on {{ a.narratives.min_days }} or more days in a row becomes a narrative: it gets a <a href="{{ root }}narratives/">page of its own</a> that tells it day by day, and it is listed on the front page while it is ongoing (covered today or yesterday). A story keeps the same identity as long as new articles keep joining it, so a narrative follows one story, not a topic. Days count in Stockholm time; news and commentary count, newsletters that summarize the day don't.</p>
  <p>Each day shows how many outlets wrote about it, the stance of their articles and the average, and up to {{ a.narratives.headlines_per_day }} headlines from different outlets: the ones that share the most words with the day's other headlines, with a different stance each where possible. When the story was on the front page that morning, its line from the front page is there too. The line at the top compares the first and the latest day with at least three confident labels, so a single article can't swing it. No model writes anything for a narrative; it is all assembled from the labels and headlines already on the site.</p>
  <p>Days before collection started come only from what the feeds still listed on the first day, so they are marked as partial.</p>
  <p>Each narrative has a seal of its own, an engraving in the manner of the fine line work on banknotes, drawn from the narrative: a lattice of hairline strings with two petals for every day, tinted from the inside out by the stance of all its articles, critical (umber) in the middle to positive (sage) at the petal tips, each stance taking its share of the line (a very small share is kept just visible). The number of days sits in the middle. A story on the front page that is part of a narrative carries its seal on the card's corner; it leads to the narrative's page. There the seal rests without a number. When the page opens, the lattice's threads twist into a cord, and the cord pays its thread out to a pen that writes, in the Baskerville of the masthead and stroke by stroke as a calligrapher would, how long the story has run: the number of days, then "day", then "narrative", and last the Q of Qaiet Times. Behind the pen, fine threads trace each letter's outline, the letter appears as a fine engraved hatch, and the color fuses in between its lines: the same stance colors, critical at the left to positive at the right, like the stripe on a card. After each word its ink lifts into the threads of its outline, which wind back into the cord; after the Q, the lattice settles and keeps turning slowly. Hover over the seal (or click it) to see it again. It stays still if your system asks for reduced motion.</p>
</section>

<section id="periods">
  <h2>Time periods</h2>
  <p>You can look at the last {% for p in a.periods %}{{ p.label }}{% if not loop.last %}{{ ", " if loop.revindex > 2 else " or " }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}. Each view ranks stories only by what happened inside it. In the longer views, a story that went quiet and came back is shown as one.</p>
  {% if a.data_since %}<p>Collection started on {{ a.data_since_long }}. Until enough time has passed, the longer views say how much they actually cover.</p>{% endif %}
</section>

<section id="limits">
  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Labels are machine-made from a headline and a few lines of summary. A headline can frame a story differently from the article under it.</li>
    <li>The outlet list decides what you see. It has {{ a.counts.outlets }} news outlets in {{ a.counts.countries }} countries and {{ a.counts.languages }} languages{% if a.regions %} ({% for r in a.regions if r.n %}{{ r.name }} {{ r.n }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}){% endif %}. Only outlets with a free feed that allow automated readers can be on it, and many large outlets in Africa, the Gulf and South Asia turn such readers away, so each region leans toward the outlets that don't. Some publishers (Reuters, AP, CNN, Chinese state media) are only visible through a secondary index with headlines alone. State-aligned outlets are marked in the list of sources.</li>
    <li>Headlines in other languages are translated by a small language model for display and for grouping; the labels are made from the original words.</li>
    <li>The overall index counts each outlet once, so the region with the most outlets weighs most{% if a.us_share %}: on {{ a.us_share.day }}, {{ a.us_share.us }} of the {{ a.us_share.n }} news outlets in it were American{% endif %}. A region or country gets its own figure only on days when at least {{ a.sentiment.min_outlets }} of its outlets were labeled.{% if a.waiting %} {{ a.waiting.n }} outlet{{ "s" if a.waiting.n != 1 }} in a language the site has only just started reading {{ "are" if a.waiting.n != 1 else "is" }} labeled and shown already, but join{{ "s" if a.waiting.n == 1 }} the index on {{ a.waiting.until }}.{% endif %}</li>
    <li>Grouping is automatic. Occasionally two related stories are split, or a loosely related piece joins a story.</li>
    <li>Social signals come from Bluesky and Hugging Face. X and Reddit are never read directly (both ask automated readers to stay away); their posts and threads show up only where the AINews recap links them, so they reflect what AINews picks.</li>
    <li>GitHub stars can be bought. Obvious clone spam (the same project name from many accounts) is filtered out, but a single inflated repository can still show up in Rising.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="privacy">
  <h2>Privacy and cost</h2>
  <p>No ads, no cookies, no analytics, no tracking. The page loads nothing from other sites: no web fonts, no images, no scripts from elsewhere. The only script is a few lines for the filters and the light/dark switch, and the page works without it. If you switch to dark, that choice is kept in your browser's local storage, and for the length of a visit the page remembers that it already showed its opening count; neither is ever sent anywhere.</p>
  <p>The language models cost money. Spending is capped at ${{ a.budget|int }} a month; this month so far: ${{ "%.2f"|format(a.spend) }}. If the cap is reached, stories and links keep updating and the labels pause until the next month. Each label records the prompt version that produced it: currently relevance <code>{{ a.prompts.relevance }}</code>, article labels <code>{{ a.prompts.news }}</code>, project labels <code>{{ a.prompts.builder }}</code>, summaries <code>{{ a.prompts.summary }}</code> and the daily line <code>{{ a.prompts.weather }}</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="health">
  <h2>Source health</h2>
  {% if a.health %}
  <p>Last collection, at {{ a.health.at_local }}: {{ a.health.ok }} of {{ a.health.total }} sources answered normally{% if a.health.failing %}, {{ a.health.failing|length }} did not{% endif %}.</p>
  {% if a.health.failing %}
  <ul class="health">
    {% for f in a.health.failing %}<li>{{ f.name }}: {{ f.status }}{% if f.error %} <span class="country">({{ f.error[:80] }})</span>{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  {% endif %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No collection has run yet.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
</main>

<footer class="colophon">
  <p><a href="{{ root }}">Back to today's overview</a></p>
</footer>

</div>
<script src="{{ root }}static/app.js" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
